WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.920
UCSF Chimera is an innovative molecular&nbsp;
graphics system with animation capabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.040
This series of tutorials will help you to&nbsp;
become proficient in using this software.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.960
Before beginning to work with Chimera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.920
we must first understand the macromolecule data&nbsp;
for a given entry on the Protein Data Bank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:27.200
Here we have PDB entry 1hys, the crystal structure&nbsp;
of a reverse transcriptase enzyme from HIV.&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:32.880
If you scroll down on the structure summary&nbsp;
page, you'll find the macromolecules section.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.560
Here, each chain in the structure is given a&nbsp;
letter code. We see here the first chain is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:49.120
given the code C. However, in the brackets we see&nbsp;
that it says "auth A" — this means that the author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.480
of the study has labeled this chain as&nbsp;
A. It's better to pay attention to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.520
labeling in these brackets because that is&nbsp;
the labeling that you will see in Chimera.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:07.440
Similarly, in the nucleic acids section, the&nbsp;
nucleotide chains are given codes in the same way.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.080
UCSF Chimera is available for free download for&nbsp;
students and educators. Following the link below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.320
you will come to the Chimera software home&nbsp;
page. Under Download, you will see the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.600
versions of the software listed. Choose the&nbsp;
one that is most applicable for your device.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.920
Upon opening UCSF Chimera, you will be greeted&nbsp;
with a black graphics window with menus on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:41.280
upper left hand corner. In the bottom right&nbsp;
hand corner, you can use the lightning bolt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.040
icon to navigate to the blue window, which is the&nbsp;
window you will see upon each subsequent opening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.080
On the right hand side, a list of&nbsp;
previously visualized files is shown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.520
To open any of these files, simply click on it.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:04.080
To import a new structure from the PDB into&nbsp;
Chimera, go to File, Fetch by ID, and type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.160
in the desired PDB identifier. Chimera will then&nbsp;
fetch the coordinates and display the structure on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.720
screen. Notice how some parts of the protein are&nbsp;
represented by ribbons and others — the ligands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:23.040
and specific residues — are represented by sticks.&nbsp;
We'll explore representations further later on.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.280
With the left mouse button pressed down, you&nbsp;
can rotate the structure about the X and Y axes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.840
The same motion done far away from the&nbsp;
structure will rotate it about the Z axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:43.280
With the right mouse button pressed down, you can&nbsp;
zoom the structure in and out. And with the middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.680
mouse button pressed down, you can translate the&nbsp;
structure across the screen. For users with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.920
mouse that only has two buttons, such as an Apple&nbsp;
Magic Mouse, you can use the left mouse button and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.640
the Option key to achieve the same translation.&nbsp;
Also, holding down the Shift key while performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.720
any of these operations makes the structure&nbsp;
10 times less sensitive to the operation.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:17.440
Once you have found a view that you like, you&nbsp;
can go to File, Save Image, and give your image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:24.320
a suitable title. If you would like a transparent&nbsp;
background, remember to choose the file type png&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.440
and a transparent background. When uploading to&nbsp;
YouTube, the standard high definition resolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:38.800
is 1280 by 720 pixels. To set these dimensions&nbsp;
for your image, uncheck this button here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:49.840
type in 1280 by 720. As a side note, images for&nbsp;
print publication require 300 pixels per inch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:57.760
of high quality image content. So if you need&nbsp;
to print an image that is, say, 7 by 4 inches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:06.160
your dimensions — that is, your resolution&nbsp;
— would need to be 2100 by 1200 pixels. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:12.160
with all these settings configured, you can save&nbsp;
the image to a suitable location on your computer.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.800
You can also save your session using&nbsp;
this option here, which will retain all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.960
of the current settings on your structure.&nbsp;
A saved session can be restored or reopened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.600
using this option. Finally, when you&nbsp;
are done working with your structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.400
you can close the session or&nbsp;
quit out of the program entirely.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.720
One other important thing to understand&nbsp;
when you are learning to visualize proteins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.640
is the difference between the asymmetric&nbsp;
unit and the biological assembly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:54.000
What I have here on screen is the PDB structure&nbsp;
5ire, which is the structure of the Zika virus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.960
capsid. Now if you know anything about virus&nbsp;
structure, you'll probably realize that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:05.840
doesn't look much like a virus. That's because&nbsp;
this is the asymmetric unit. To find the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:16.800
biological assembly, go to Favorites, Model Panel,&nbsp;
and click Biological Unit. Now the entire viral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:25.360
structure comes up and if we zoom out, we can see&nbsp;
that it is indeed a fully functional viral capsid.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.800
You can also see this distinction on the entry's&nbsp;
Structure Summary page on rcsb.org. By default,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.480
the complete biological assembly is shown&nbsp;
here, but upon clicking one of these arrows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:48.400
we see the asymmetric unit that we had when&nbsp;
we first rendered the structure in Chimera.

